---
// src/pages/en/self-hosting/configuration-generator.astro
//
// Hosts the client-side Configuration Generator island inside the standard
// Starlight docs chrome (sidebar, header). The tool itself is a self-
// contained Vue component that reads the vendored config JSON schemas and a
// preset manifest to generate config.yaml / auth.yaml / .env entirely in the
// browser, with no dependency on the running application.
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import ConfigGenerator from '../../../components/config-generator/ConfigGenerator.vue';

const title = 'Configuration Generator';
const description =
  'Choose settings for your self-hosted Onetime Secret installation, then ' +
  'download starting configuration files and follow the setup steps.';
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title,
    description,
    tableOfContents: false,
    lastUpdated: false,
  }}>
  <p class="cg-intro">
    Choose settings for your self-hosted Onetime Secret installation. The generated
    files update as you make changes. This tool covers common choices; other
    settings use the application’s defaults.
  </p>
  <p class="cg-intro">
    The settings below are based on Onetime Secret <strong>v0.26.18</strong>. Have your installation method and datastore connection details ready.
    You won’t enter passwords or API keys here: add those values on your server
    after downloading the files. You can bookmark or share this page to save your choices.
  </p>

  <ConfigGenerator client:load />

  <section aria-labelledby="apply-configuration">
    <h2 id="apply-configuration">Apply your configuration</h2>
    <p>
      These files are a starting point for the options above. They do not install
      databases, configure an identity provider, or replace the setup steps for your <a href="/en/install/docker/">Docker</a> or <a href="/en/install/linux/">Linux</a> installation.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Save the YAML settings.</strong> For a new source installation,
        save the generated files as <code>etc/config.yaml</code> and <code>etc/auth.yaml</code> in the application directory. The application merges these settings with
        its shipped defaults. For an existing installation, back up your files and
        merge the generated keys into the matching sections; preserve your other
        settings and avoid duplicate YAML keys. Leave <code>etc/defaults/</code> unchanged.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Complete your environment.</strong> Add the generated entries to
        your existing server environment or <code>.env</code> file, filling in
        blank values and preserving existing secrets. Full authentication mode
        needs a SQL database connection and the independent <code>AUTH_SECRET</code>, <code>ARGON2_SECRET</code>, and <code>ACCOUNT_ID_SECRET</code> values.
        The <a href="/en/install/docker/#what-must-be-set-before-either-stack-starts">Docker setup guide</a> explains secret generation and additional Compose requirements, including <code>VALKEY_PASSWORD</code>. Keep the completed file private and out of version control.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Finish any provider or network setup.</strong> For SSO, add the
        variables for the provider you use; the SSO switch alone does not configure
        a provider. For multiple regions, set <code>JURISDICTION</code> to this
        deployment’s identifier and <code>JURISDICTIONS</code> to a list such as <code>EU:eu.example.com,CA:ca.example.com</code>. Set <code>VALKEY_URL</code> or <code>REDIS_URL</code> for your datastore; requiring TLS also needs a
        TLS-capable server and a <code>valkeys://</code> or <code>rediss://</code> URL.
        See the <a href="/en/self-hosting/environment-variables/">environment variable reference</a> for provider credentials and connection settings.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Make the files available to the application.</strong> A source
        installation reads YAML from its application directory; ensure its service
        also receives your environment values. In Docker, host files are not
        automatically available inside the container. Bind-mount your YAML files
        at <code>/app/etc/config.yaml</code> and <code>/app/etc/auth.yaml</code> for the application and any workers using them. Use read-only file mounts
        and retain existing data volumes. Follow your installation guide to pass
        environment values to each service; the generator’s <code>.env</code> snippet is not a complete Compose environment.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Reload and check the result.</strong> Restart the application and
        workers after changing their configuration. Recreate Docker containers
        when changing their environment or mounts. Follow <a href="/en/install/verify/">Verify your install</a>, then create and
        retrieve a test secret and try any sign-in method you enabled.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      For settings beyond this tool, use the <a href="/en/self-hosting/configuration/">configuration reference</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</StarlightPage>

<style is:global>
  /* The two-column tool needs more room than a prose page. Scoped with :has()
     so only a page that renders the generator gets the wider column. */
  :root:has(.cg) {
    --sl-content-width: 66rem;
  }
  .cg-intro {
    max-width: 45rem;
  }
</style>
